# Performance

Avoid premature optimization. But when a screen feels slow, do these in order.

## Re-renders

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders. Subscribe Zustand stores with selectors:
  `useStore(s => s.field)`, not `useStore()`.
- Memoize expensive derived values with `useMemo`. Memoize callbacks passed to
  memoized children with `useCallback`.
- Use `React.memo` on pure presentational components in hot paths only.
- The React Compiler (`reactCompiler: true`) handles most cases — measure before
  hand-memoizing.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — selector returns new object every render, every store update re-renders
const { phone, isPending } = useOtpFlowStore();

// ✅ GOOD — narrow subscriptions
const phone = useOtpFlowStore(s => s.phone);
const isPending = useOtpFlowStore(s => s.isPending);
```

## Lists

- Use stable keys; never `index` for dynamic data.
- Prefer `FlashList` for large datasets (> ~50 items or async-loaded feeds).
- For `FlatList`: set `keyExtractor`, `getItemLayout` (when fixed height),
  `removeClippedSubviews`, `windowSize`, `initialNumToRender`.
- Avoid inline arrow functions inside `renderItem` for large lists — hoist them.

## Inputs & gestures

- Debounce expensive user actions (search, autosave) — typically 250–400ms.
- Throttle high-frequency events (scroll, drag) — typically 16ms (single frame).

## Heavy work

- Move heavy work off the JS thread (Reanimated worklets, native modules).
- Defer non-critical work with `InteractionManager.runAfterInteractions`.
